WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.103
One of these $100
bills is real,

00:00:03.103 --> 00:00:07.440
and the other one is a piece 
of prop money made for movies.

00:00:07.474 --> 00:00:09.642
Can you tell which
one is which?

00:00:09.676 --> 00:00:12.979
They both have a blue security
ribbon, textured ink,

00:00:13.012 --> 00:00:15.315
and even the 
smallest detail,

00:00:15.348 --> 00:00:19.152
like text from the 
Declaration of Independence.

00:00:19.152 --> 00:00:22.722
The answer is obvious 
when you flip them over.

00:00:23.823 --> 00:00:25.625
Bills as detailed
as this one

00:00:25.658 --> 00:00:28.428
are required to be blank
on the other side.

00:00:28.428 --> 00:00:30.230
That's because 
if prop makers,

00:00:30.263 --> 00:00:32.499
like Prop Movie 
Money in Florida,

00:00:32.532 --> 00:00:35.168
print money that
looks too real,

00:00:35.168 --> 00:00:38.938
they risk getting in trouble
with the US government.

00:00:38.972 --> 00:00:41.007
That's what happened
20 years ago

00:00:41.007 --> 00:00:43.309
on the set of "Rush Hour 2."

00:00:43.309 --> 00:00:45.412
Gregg Bilson Jr.: 
Money is more work

00:00:45.445 --> 00:00:47.213
than it's worth,
in the long run.

00:00:47.247 --> 00:00:49.182
Narrator: That's 
Gregg Bilson Jr.,

00:00:49.182 --> 00:00:53.052
CEO of the Los 
Angeles-based ISS Props,

00:00:53.052 --> 00:00:55.955
one of the biggest prop
houses in the world.

00:00:55.989 --> 00:00:59.592
In 2000, Greg got an 
order for and printed

00:00:59.592 --> 00:01:04.097
$1 trillion of prop 
money for "Rush Hour 2,"

00:01:04.130 --> 00:01:07.734
most of which was going 
to be blown up on screen.

00:01:07.767 --> 00:01:10.737
And the prop money looks
good in this scene.

00:01:10.770 --> 00:01:11.805
Too good, in fact.

00:01:11.805 --> 00:01:13.006
Carter: Hello, Benjamin!

00:01:13.039 --> 00:01:15.141
Narrator: The fake 
money looked so real

00:01:15.141 --> 00:01:18.378
that some extras on set
pocketed some of it

00:01:18.411 --> 00:01:20.947
and tried to spend
it at real stores.

00:01:20.947 --> 00:01:23.650
That alerted the 
US Secret Service,

00:01:23.683 --> 00:01:27.020
which confiscated and 
destroyed the fake bills

00:01:27.020 --> 00:01:30.090
and the digital files
used to print them.

00:01:30.090 --> 00:01:34.661
It had cost $100,000 to print
all of that fake money,

00:01:34.694 --> 00:01:37.664
so losing all of it 
was a financial blow

00:01:37.697 --> 00:01:39.999
to Gregg and ISS.

00:01:39.999 --> 00:01:43.636
Bilson: We didn't try to make 
fake money to dupe the public.

00:01:43.670 --> 00:01:46.039
We made fake money
to make a movie.

00:01:46.072 --> 00:01:47.740
But we just made 
the prop too good.

00:01:47.740 --> 00:01:50.243
Narrator: The "Rush Hour
2" incident underscored

00:01:50.276 --> 00:01:53.079
an obvious dilemma for
printing fake money.

00:01:53.112 --> 00:01:55.915
The money needs to look
realistic on camera,

00:01:55.915 --> 00:01:59.052
but it can't look too
realistic up close,

00:01:59.085 --> 00:02:02.288
or people might try to
spend it in real life.

00:02:02.322 --> 00:02:06.059
The problem has become even
greater in recent years,

00:02:06.059 --> 00:02:09.329
with better cameras capturing
more and more details

00:02:09.362 --> 00:02:11.231
from the background
of scenes.

00:02:11.231 --> 00:02:13.299
So the prop industry
has come up with

00:02:13.333 --> 00:02:15.902
two different types
of prop money,

00:02:15.902 --> 00:02:19.205
each with its own 
strengths and weaknesses.

00:02:19.205 --> 00:02:22.242
For scenes where the cash
is filmed from far away,

00:02:22.275 --> 00:02:26.112
productions typically use
standard-grade bills.

00:02:26.112 --> 00:02:30.350
The iconic money scene from 
"Breaking Bad" uses these bills,

00:02:30.350 --> 00:02:33.319
which were rented 
from Gregg and ISS.

00:02:33.353 --> 00:02:35.922
These bills look
real from afar,

00:02:35.922 --> 00:02:38.791
but up close are
obviously fake,

00:02:38.825 --> 00:02:40.627
with lots of 
clear differences

00:02:40.627 --> 00:02:43.196
when compared to
the real thing.

00:02:43.196 --> 00:02:45.765
The portrait on the 
bill is poor quality

00:02:45.798 --> 00:02:47.300
compared to the real one.

00:02:47.333 --> 00:02:49.736
And instead of "one
hundred dollars,"

00:02:49.736 --> 00:02:51.771
it just reads "one hundred."

00:02:51.804 --> 00:02:55.208
The smaller details on the
bill are also altered.

00:02:55.208 --> 00:02:57.410
The seals are a 
different design,

00:02:57.443 --> 00:03:00.313
and the signatures on the
bill have been changed.

00:03:00.313 --> 00:03:02.615
Then there are the
obvious additions,

00:03:02.649 --> 00:03:04.384
like the prominently
displayed

00:03:04.384 --> 00:03:06.619
"for motion 
picture use only."

00:03:06.619 --> 00:03:10.623
In fact, if you zoom in to this
scene from "Breaking Bad,"

00:03:10.657 --> 00:03:13.226
while it's blurry, 
you can actually see

00:03:13.226 --> 00:03:17.497
that each bill says "for 
motion picture use only."

00:03:17.564 --> 00:03:21.067
These changes should keep
the Secret Service away,

00:03:21.100 --> 00:03:23.870
but they also mean 
standard fake bills

00:03:23.870 --> 00:03:25.872
are no good for close-ups.

00:03:25.905 --> 00:03:30.610
So for those shots, movies will
often use high-grade bills.

00:03:30.610 --> 00:03:33.846
High-grade bills are 
identical to real money

00:03:33.880 --> 00:03:36.649
but are printed 
on only one side

00:03:36.649 --> 00:03:39.686
so that they can't be 
confused with the real thing.

00:03:39.686 --> 00:03:41.521
Like the one we 
showed you earlier,

00:03:41.521 --> 00:03:45.291
which was printed by 
RJR Props in Atlanta.

00:03:45.325 --> 00:03:48.061
You can see an example
of a high-grade bill

00:03:48.094 --> 00:03:52.932
in this scene from the 2014
movie "Let's Be Cops."

00:03:52.932 --> 00:03:55.735
An alternate solution
that Gregg and ISS

00:03:55.768 --> 00:03:58.137
have been using for 
the past several years

00:03:58.171 --> 00:04:00.873
is to simply use real bills.

00:04:00.873 --> 00:04:03.443
This is the first 
version that they offer.

00:04:03.476 --> 00:04:06.913
ISS will take a pile of 
completely blank bill notes

00:04:06.913 --> 00:04:09.949
and then place one real bill
on the top of the stack

00:04:09.983 --> 00:04:11.451
and one on the bottom,

00:04:11.451 --> 00:04:13.953
making it appear as
if the entire stack

00:04:13.953 --> 00:04:16.222
is full of real bills.

00:04:16.255 --> 00:04:18.791
The second method for
using real dollars

00:04:18.791 --> 00:04:23.196
has a bigger risk attached, 
but it may be the best option.

00:04:23.229 --> 00:04:26.399
An entire stack
of real bills.

00:04:26.399 --> 00:04:30.637
ISS will get stacks of
$10,000 from the bank

00:04:30.670 --> 00:04:32.572
and then deliver
them to set.

00:04:32.605 --> 00:04:35.141
While having this much
cash lying around

00:04:35.141 --> 00:04:37.377
makes some 
productions nervous,

00:04:37.410 --> 00:04:39.445
it looks great on film

00:04:39.445 --> 00:04:43.249
and eliminates any risk 
with the Secret Service.

00:04:43.283 --> 00:04:46.686
That's what they did in
this shot from "Ozark."

00:04:46.686 --> 00:04:49.322
Bilson: I see fake money
used all the time,

00:04:49.355 --> 00:04:52.358
and I think it is appalling,

00:04:52.358 --> 00:04:54.327
because I'm a 
property master,

00:04:54.327 --> 00:04:55.895
and I want things
to be authentic

00:04:55.928 --> 00:04:58.064
and accurate and look right.

00:04:58.064 --> 00:04:58.931
Narrator: Take
a look at this

00:04:58.965 --> 00:05:01.768
season one episode
of "Girlfriends."

00:05:01.768 --> 00:05:05.872
The fake-looking money is 
distracting to the audience.

00:05:05.905 --> 00:05:09.309
While productions may 
prefer to use real money,

00:05:09.342 --> 00:05:11.477
sometimes it's unavoidable,

00:05:11.477 --> 00:05:14.013
like in scenes where
bills are destroyed

00:05:14.013 --> 00:05:17.183
or in scenes that require
an absurd quantity.

00:05:17.183 --> 00:05:19.252
In cases like 
these, Gregg says

00:05:19.285 --> 00:05:21.754
that he will still
use fake money.

00:05:21.754 --> 00:05:25.224
Except he certainly doesn't 
print the fake money himself,

00:05:25.258 --> 00:05:27.293
as he still has his
cease and desist

00:05:27.293 --> 00:05:28.961
from the Secret Service.

00:05:28.961 --> 00:05:31.931
So he buys it from
Prop Movie Money,

00:05:31.931 --> 00:05:35.368
one of the few printers
that make prop money.

00:05:35.401 --> 00:05:38.104
The ultimate irony of
printing prop money

00:05:38.104 --> 00:05:40.873
is that it actually 
isn't very profitable.

00:05:40.907 --> 00:05:42.975
Standard and 
high-grade bills

00:05:43.009 --> 00:05:47.880
sell for roughly $45 for
a stack of 100 bills.

00:05:47.880 --> 00:05:51.250
Gregg still has two 
bills from "Rush Hour 2"

00:05:51.284 --> 00:05:53.986
that the Secret Service
didn't confiscate.

00:05:53.986 --> 00:05:56.823
Even though these bills
look less realistic

00:05:56.823 --> 00:05:58.458
than modern prop bills,

00:05:58.491 --> 00:06:01.027
he still has them 
encased in plastic

00:06:01.027 --> 00:06:04.263
so that no one can try to
use them in real life.

00:06:04.263 --> 00:06:08.067
They're a physical reminder 
of the risks prop makers take

00:06:08.101 --> 00:06:12.772
and the rewards they reap to
get that perfect money shot.

